XXXX. Violation of FCRA Seven-Year Rule Under 15 U.S. Code 1681c ( a ) ( 4 ), derogatory accounts must be removed after seven years from the original delinquency date, not from the date of charge-off. Your reporting attempts to illegally re-age the debt by assigning a charge-off date over two years after the delinquency began. This is not only deceptive and abusive, but it also directly violates federal law.

3. Violation of TILA Improper Assessment of Delinquency Per 15 U.S. Code 1666b, a credit card issuer must provide a minimum of 21 days to make payment before applying late fees or reporting the account as delinquent. There is no record that this account met that statutory requirement before the 30-day late payment was reported in XX/XX/XXXX. If such records exist, I formally demand they be produced. Otherwise, this is a clear violation of federal law, and any late reporting is unlawful. 



4. Material Harm This inaccurate reporting has had devastating consequences on my financial life. Due to the falsified late payment history and improper charge-off date, I have been : Denied credit on multiple occasions, Subjected to higher interest rates, and Restricted from qualifying for government and law enforcement roles, as many federal positions conduct background and credit reviews. 

